problem:
Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.

$\frac{-6}{3+2\text{i}}=($
 {{field-1}} 
$)\,+\,($
{{field-2}}
$)\,\text{i}$

examples.worked_solution:
The denominator is $-1+2\textrm{i}$ and its conjugate is $-1-2\textrm{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-1-2\textrm{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:

$$
\eqalign{
		\dfrac {4} {-1 +2\textrm{i}} 
&=\dfrac {4} {-1 +2 \textrm{i}} \cdot \dfrac {-1 -2 \textrm{i}} {-1-2\textrm{i}}  \[5pt]
&= \dfrac {-4 - 8 \textrm{i} } {(-1)^2 - (2\textrm{i})^2}  \[5pt]
&= \dfrac {-4 - 8 \textrm{i} } {1 - 4\textrm{i}^2}  \[5pt]
&= \dfrac {-4 - 8 \textrm{i} } {1 - 4(-1)}  \[5pt]
&= \dfrac {-4 - 8 \textrm{i} } {5} 
	}
$$

Now that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b \textrm{i}\mathbin{:}$

$$
\eqalign{
		\dfrac {-4 - 8 \textrm{i} } {5} =
		-\dfrac{4} {5} - \dfrac {8} {5} \textrm{i}	
	}
$$
